  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 7NT. South must take 7 club tricks. Normally 10 card suits missing the Queen do not provide much scope in the play. However, the blockage of this suit gives South some options.\n\nSouth wins the opening lead and leads a club out of his hand. When West follows low, South must decide what to play from dummy. If West has at least 2 clubs including the ♣Q, South must finesse on the first round. When East cannot win this trick running the clubs will be easy.\n\nPlaying the ♣K or ♣A on the first round of the suit is essentially playing East for a singleton ♣Q. If the ♣Q is guarded once, South will go down after cashing a top honour. The other top club honour will drop the ♣Q, but the suit will be blocked.\n\nIf West has all 3 clubs, South will still go down after cashing a top honour. South can cross back to his hand and play a club. West will cover and the suit will be blocked.\n\nFinessing on the first round of clubs is easily the best play. As the saying goes, 8 ever, 9 never, 10 -- on this deal.",
